Alembic owns all application schema changes.

One migration chain under `migrations/versions` serves the configured database, the Selected dataset
and the Working dataset. The single head is `20261103_0023`.

`uv run alembic upgrade head` applies the head to the database that the settings and the
environment file name. `migrations/env.py` composes the tracked TOML and the environment file
through `load_raw_database_settings`, and it accepts a caller-owned connection through
`config.attributes["connection"]`, so a caller can reuse one resolved connection instead of
reopening settings. With no supplied connection the default settings and engine path runs.

An enrolled site resolves the Selected dataset. Enroll it with
`uv run python scripts/admin.py setup datasets`, then apply the head to it with
`uv run python scripts/admin.py migrate selected`. The command also accepts `working` and
`database`, and the `database` form applies only while no Selected dataset is enrolled. Each form
requires the `db` Compose service to run.

Reverse one revision with `uv run alembic downgrade <revision>`. A downgrade that would lose
retained evidence raises an error.

The feedback application store has its own chain under `migrations_feedback/versions`, with the
single head `20261103_feedback_0004`. Apply it with
`uv run alembic -c alembic_feedback.ini upgrade head`, and `migrations_feedback/env.py` resolves the
feedback database through `load_feedback_settings` with the same caller-owned connection hook.
Every downgrade in that chain raises an error instead of dropping retained answers, attachments or
publication outcomes. A reverse step therefore restores from a dump.
